# 1. Executive Summary

During the security assessment, a **Web Cache Poisoning** vulnerability was identified due to the unsafe use of the **X-Forwarded-Host** HTTP header.

The application trusted this user-controlled header to dynamically generate absolute URLs within the HTML response. Because the caching layer did not include this header as part of its cache key (an **unkeyed header**), an attacker could poison the cache with a malicious response that would subsequently be served to other users.

By exploiting this behavior, it was possible to replace the legitimate JavaScript resource (`tracking.js`) with a malicious version hosted on an attacker-controlled server, resulting in arbitrary JavaScript execution in victims' browsers.

